                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "When you start the game, the first thing you see is some broken HTML and an error message.  After a brief party creation step, you enter the game, and are treated to some dialogue between your characters, which is a nice touch.  Once you get going, though, you notice the sluggish and unresponsive controls that make just moving around, managing your inventory, and interacting with the environment a chore.  Then, a little ways in, you encounter your first opponent: a rat, which you'll fight on a narrow platform with nowhere to go.\n\nThese rats take upwards of twenty hits to kill (and your attacks can still miss), but they can easily kill one of you in just a few.  That's not too out of bounds for Dungeon Master-likes, since you're meant to move around in combat to avoid getting hit - but here, you can't do that.  While any of your attacks are on cooldown, which lasts about five seconds, you are rooted to the spot.  You can deal extra damage from behind, you can attack from range, you can run away to recover, you can do this or do that - but all of these tactics are hindered by the fact that you can't move after striking, even if movement control itself wasn't already compromised.  This is simply not enjoyable combat design, and I can't understand why it was made this way.  Eye of the Beholder was primitive, but played much more smoothly.\n\nIt took me three attempts to kill the first rat without losing someone (KOed characters recover, but they don't get XP, so it still matters).  After two more tiresome battles with rats, I decided I didn't want to continue.  It just doesn't feel good to play.",
